{
  "image_name": "423ed399abce912f059720c0022d9b6a.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how diverse waste streams can be converted into useful products through microbial engineering. It groups feedstocks into horticultural waste, animal agricultural waste, food waste, and one-carbon waste. These sources contain different major components: plant wastes are rich in cellulose, hemicellulose, lignin, starch, and glycogen; animal wastes provide lactose, fatty acids, and keratin; food waste contains carbohydrates, proteins, and lipids; and one-carbon waste includes gases or simple molecules such as CO, CO2, methane, methanol, formaldehyde, and formate.\n\nAll of these materials are shown feeding into engineered microbial platforms. The central idea is that microbes can be redesigned to process chemically distinct waste constituents by three broad strategies: rewiring metabolic pathways, introducing heterologous enzymes with new functions, and using adaptive laboratory evolution to improve performance. The pathway-rewiring sketch emphasizes redirecting substrate flow toward desired products, while the enzyme and evolution panels highlight expanding microbial capabilities and selecting better strains.\n\nAt the bottom, the figure shows the shared outcome of this approach: waste valorization into higher-value products, including biofuels, biosurfactants, bioplastics, food additives, and pharmaceuticals. Overall, it presents a broad waste-to-bioproduct framework.",
